      "content": "Proponents of segregation in the 1920s often relied on the concept of \"separate but equal,\" arguing that distinct social structures were necessary for maintaining social order. They frequently invoked pseudo-scientific ideas about inherent racial differences, claiming that mixing races would lead to societal decay or moral corruption. Furthermore, many justifications centered on protecting the perceived cultural purity and traditions of the white community. These arguments were deeply rooted in a belief system that prioritized racial hierarchy.",
