      "content": "I see. So it wasn't just about being \"mean,\" it was about these supposedly \"objective\" observations of the time. To make this really helpful for the debate, could you go a bit deeper into how they framed the \"social stability\" argument? For instance, if a student is arguing that segregation was actually a \"preventative measure\" for social unrest or crime, what would be the most \"logical\" sociological points they would make based on the thinking of that 1920s era?",
